---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import {
  INACTIVITY_DAYS,
  LEGAL_UPDATED,
  NOT_AFFILIATED,
  OPERATOR_EMAIL,
  OPERATOR_NAME,
  TRAKT_CONNECTED_APPS_URL,
} from '../data/site';

// Limits of the hosted instance. They must equal RATE_LIMIT_READS and RATE_LIMIT_WRITES on the
// deployed server; these are the server defaults.
const READS_PER_MINUTE = 120;
const WRITES_PER_MINUTE = 30;
// Owner: these terms are a plain-language draft written from how the service behaves.
// They are not legal advice. Have a lawyer read them if you want certainty.
// (A JS comment: an HTML comment would be published in the page.)
---

<Legal
  title="Terms of service"
  description="Terms of service of the hosted Trakt MCP service: who can use it, what you authorize, acceptable use, liability, and the governing law."
  path="/terms"
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <h2>Who provides the service</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided by {OPERATOR_NAME}, an individual (the operator). It connects an AI assistant you choose, such
    as Claude or ChatGPT, to your Trakt account. Contact: <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR_EMAIL}`}>{OPERATOR_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>{NOT_AFFILIATED}</p>

  <h2>Who can use it</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone with a Trakt account can connect. Some Trakt features need Trakt VIP, and Trakt applies its own limits to lists,
    watchlists and collections; this service passes them on and does not work around them. The operator can refuse an
    account that breaks these terms.
  </p>

  <h2>What you authorize</h2>
  <p>
    When you connect an assistant, you let it act on your Trakt account through the Trakt API: browse the catalog, read
    your watchlist, history, collection and ratings, and change them, check in to what you are watching, and manage
    recommendations. Trakt offers no narrower permission, so the access covers your whole Trakt account. You stay
    responsible for what you ask it to do. A history entry, rating or list item removed through the service is removed at
    Trakt and cannot be restored by this service, so keep your own export of your Trakt data.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Stay within the limits: {READS_PER_MINUTE} reading calls and {WRITES_PER_MINUTE} writing calls per user per minute.
    </li>
    <li>Do not try to reach another person's data, or to disrupt the service.</li>
    <li>Follow Trakt's terms and API use policy, and the terms of your assistant's provider.</li>
    <li>Follow the law.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The service is provided as is</h2>
  <p>
    The service is free and comes with no promise of availability, of speed, or of backups. It can change, be interrupted
    or be shut down. If it is shut down, a notice appears on this website. Data changed by an assistant can be lost or
    overwritten by mistake.
  </p>

  <h2>Ending your use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You can stop at any time: disconnect the connector in your assistant, revoke the application in Trakt under
      Settings, <a href={TRAKT_CONNECTED_APPS_URL}>Connected Apps</a>, or ask the operator to disconnect you.
    </li>
    <li>The operator can suspend an account that breaks these terms or harms the service.</li>
    <li>An account unused for {INACTIVITY_DAYS} days is purged automatically.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What is kept about you, and for how long, is in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    As far as the law allows, the operator is not liable for indirect damage, or for the loss of data or of viewing
    history, ratings or lists, that results from using the service. Nothing here limits rights that the law does not allow
    to be limited, such as the rights of consumers.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    The date at the top of this page shows the last change. If you keep using the service after a change, you accept the
    new terms. If you do not, disconnect your account.
  </p>

  <h2>Governing law</h2>
  <p>These terms are governed by French law.</p>
</Legal>
